  "html": "<p><strong>Founded:</strong> 1907<br /><strong>Club colours:</strong> Red &amp; Black<br /><strong>Major honours:</strong><br /><u>Champions of Malta</u>: 1913/14, 1917/18, 1946/47, 1982/83, 1986/87, 1987/88, 1990/91, 2020/21, 2022/23 (<strong>9</strong>)<br /><u>FA Trophy Winners</u>: 1982/83, 1983/84, 1986/87, 1987/88, 1988/89, 1991/92 (<strong>6</strong>)<br /><u>1st Division Champions</u>: 1999-2000, 2004/05, 2006/07 (<strong>3</strong>)<br /><u>2<sup>nd</sup> Division Champions (2<sup>nd</sup> level</u>): 1921/22, 1937/38, 1975/76 (<strong>2</strong>)<br /><u>Super Cup</u>: 1986/87, 1987/88, 1988/89, 1990/91, 1991/92, 2022/23 (<strong>6</strong>)<br /><u>Cassar Cup</u>: 1946/47, 1948/49 (<strong>2</strong>)<br /><u>Euro/Lowenbrau Cup</u>: 1985/86, 1988/89, 1991/92, 1992/93 (<strong>4</strong>)<br /><u>Super Five Tournament</u>: 1991/92 (<strong>1</strong>)<br /><u>2<sup>nd</sup> Division Knock-Out Cup Winners</u>: 1970/71 (<strong>1</strong>)<br /><u>2<sup>nd</sup> Division Sons of Malta Cup Winners</u>: 1976/77 (<strong>1</strong>)<br /><u>Scicluna Cup ‘B’ Winners</u>: 1950/51 (<strong>1</strong>)<br /><br />The Ħamrun Spartans returned back to glory in 2021, winning their 8<sup>th</sup> national championship after a lapse of 30 years. During the last three decades, the club went through hard times, even spending one season in the 2<sup>nd</sup> Division, at the islands’ third football tier (2014/15). The Spartans were founded in 1907 and until football was transferred to Gzira in 1922, played most of their football on home soil at the Mile End, winning two early national championships in 1914 and 1918. Ħamrun returned to the top at the end of the 40s and start of the 50s, being crowned Malta Champions in 1947, winning the prestigious Cassar Cup twice, followed by four runners-up positions in 5 years. However, from 1960, the club had to bow for Floriana, Sliema Wanderers, Valletta and Hibernians, making the team even fight against relegation, which could eventually not be avoided in 1970 after 24 consecutive years amongst the elite of Maltese football. The best years were yet to come, thanks to the late Victor Tedesco, who was elected as club president in 1979. Ħamrun Spartans dominated Maltese football from 1982 up to 1993, during a period they won no less then 20 trophies, including four national championships. In 1984, Ħamrun beat the Northern Irish of Ballymena United at their own ground in the European Cup Winners Cup (0-1), the first ever away-win for a Maltese club in any of the European Cup competitions, qualifying to the next round 3-1 on aggregate. In 1996, the club opened their own football stadium with a capacity of 6,000. The club has a successful youth nursery, which forms an integral part of the club’s set-up. <br /><br /><strong>Player Records</strong><br />Most Club Appearances: <strong>Stefan Sultana </strong>(472 matches)<br />Most Club Goals: <strong>Stefan Sultana</strong> (234 goals)<br />Most capped player for national team: <strong>Michael Degiorgio </strong>(76 caps) for Malta<br /><br /><strong>Malta Footballer of the Year Award Winners (6):</strong><br />Raymond Vella (1986/87 &amp; 1990/91), Michael Degiorgio (1989/90) &amp; Joe Brincat (1992/93), Matthew Guillaumier (2020/21 &amp; 2022/23)<br /><br /><em>records correct as at 1<sup>st</sup> January 2024</em>                </p>",
